{#
   THE FILTER PANEL — one component for all 41 admin lists (S134h).

   The format was settled in `/admin/design#filtres` after four rounds of
   comparable proposals (S130e). This file *is* that format; the mockup on the
   design page now embeds this template rather than a copy of it, so the two can
   no longer drift.

   ⚠️ **Three natures of control, three places — and only two of them are here.**
   What *creates* went up into the page header (the green named button). What
   *searches* went down into the list header, beside the count. What is left, and
   all that is left, is what *filters*:

     tier 1  one row of one-click tiles, with counts, for the facet that DEFINES
             the page. Exactly one row. If a page has no obvious defining facet,
             it has no tile row — not two rows of competing ones.
     tier 2  "Affiner": everything else, as labelled dropdowns that apply on
             change. **Sub-venue is the first of them.**

   ⚠️ **The whole panel disappears when it would be empty**, and the sub-venue
   dropdown disappears on a single-sub-venue install — a control whose only two
   choices are "all" and the only one there is cannot change anything. That test
   lives here so no caller can forget it (it was forgotten on every admin list
   before S130c).

   ⚠️ **Sub-venue is a dropdown, not a tile row (S134h).** S130c had made it a
   tile row to match the others; the review that followed concluded there can be
   only ONE tile row and it belongs to the page's defining facet. Sub-venue is a
   filter among filters on a machines list — it is not what a machines list is
   about — so it reads as the first "Affiner" menu.

   Parameters (all optional except `list_route`):

     list_route, list_route_params, list_query   where the controls point, and
                 the query they must carry so changing one never drops another
     tiles           [{label, count, query|category, active, icon_slug, label_is_key}]
     tiles_label     the row's label ("Catégorie"). Also its accessible name.
     tiles_preserve_query  merge `list_query` into each tile's link (default true)
     refine          [{name, label, value, options: [{value, label}]}] — dropdowns.
                 A field may pass `tiles:` instead of `options:` and the tile
                 list is turned into a menu here: same `{label, count, query}`
                 shape the tile rows already use, so a facet demoted out of the
                 tile row costs the caller one word and no new controller code.
     search          the current search term, re-emitted as a hidden field so
                 refining never throws away what was typed
     venue_context   the array from `VenueContext`; becomes the FIRST dropdown
     client_filter   wire the tiles to the `admin-list-filter` Stimulus controller
                     instead of navigating (the client-side lists)

   ⚠️ This template computes nothing about what a machine or a place is. Same
   rule as `_admin_list` and `_catalogue`: an `{% if %}` on entity type here and
   it has stopped being a component.
#}
{% set _query = list_query|default({}) %}
{% set _tiles = tiles|default([]) %}
{% set _refine = refine|default([]) %}
{% set _venues = venue_context|default(null) and venue_context.venues|length > 1 %}

{% if _tiles is not empty or _refine is not empty or _venues %}
    <div class="admin-filter-panel">
        {% if _tiles is not empty %}
            <div class="afp-tier">
                {% if tiles_label|default('') %}<span class="afp-tier-label" id="afp-facet-label">{{ tiles_label }}</span>{% endif %}
                <nav class="ml-cats afp-tiles"{% if tiles_label|default('') %} aria-labelledby="afp-facet-label"{% endif %}>
                    {% for tile in _tiles %}
                        {# ⚠️ A tile's query is merged INTO the current one, then
                           empties are stripped. Both halves matter: merging is
                           what stops a category tile from throwing away the
                           sub-venue and the search, and stripping is what lets a
                           tile clear the facet it owns by naming it with an
                           empty value ("Toutes" ⇒ `category: ''`). A tile that
                           merely omits its own key cannot turn its facet off. #}
                        {% set _tile_query = (tiles_preserve_query|default(true) ? _query|merge(tile.query|default({})) : tile.query|default({}))
                            |filter(value => value is not null and value != '') %}
                        <a class="ml-tile{{ tile.active|default(false) ? ' is-on' : '' }}"
                           href="{{ path(list_route, (list_route_params|default({}))|merge(_tile_query)) }}"
                           {%- if tile.active|default(false) %} aria-current="true"{% endif %}
                           {%- if client_filter|default(false) %} data-admin-list-filter-tile data-admin-list-filter-category="{{ tile.category|default('all') }}" data-action="click->admin-list-filter#category"{% endif %}>
                            {% if tile.icon_slug|default('') %}<span class="ml-tile-icon">{% include 'site/_category_icon.html.twig' with { slug: tile.icon_slug } only %}</span>{% endif %}
                            <span class="ml-tile-label">{{ tile.label_is_key|default(false) ? tile.label|trans : tile.label }}</span>
                            {% if tile.count is defined %}<span class="ml-tile-count">{{ tile.count }}</span>{% endif %}
                        </a>
                    {% endfor %}
                </nav>
            </div>
        {% endif %}

        {% if _refine is not empty or _venues %}
            {# The keys the dropdowns own. Everything else in `list_query` is
               re-emitted as a hidden input, so refining never silently drops the
               tile that is selected or the words that were searched.

               ⚠️ `page` is deliberately NOT carried. Narrowing to a filter with
               fewer rows while staying on page 4 shows an empty list, which reads
               as "there is nothing here". Every other control in this file drops
               it for the same reason. #}
            {% set _owned = ['location', 'page']|merge(_refine|map(f => f.name)) %}
            <form class="afp-tier afp-refine" method="get" action="{{ path(list_route, list_route_params|default({})) }}">
                <span class="afp-tier-label">{{ 'admin_list.refine'|trans }}</span>
                {# ⚠️ `|filter`, not `{% for … if … %}` — the inline `if` on a for
                   tag was removed in Twig 3 and fails at LINT, not at render, so
                   it takes the whole template down rather than one row. The arrow
                   receives (value, key) in that order. #}
                {% for key, value in _query|filter((value, key) => key not in _owned) %}
                    <input type="hidden" name="{{ key }}" value="{{ value }}">
                {% endfor %}
                {# The search term lives in the list header, not in this form, so
                   it is not in `list_query` on most pages. Without this, picking
                   a sub-venue silently emptied the search box. #}
                {% if search|default('') and 'q' not in _query|keys %}
                    <input type="hidden" name="q" value="{{ search }}">
                {% endif %}

                {% if _venues %}
                    <label class="afp-select{{ venue_context.location != 'all' ? ' is-set' : '' }}">
                        <span class="afp-k">{{ 'venue_context.label'|trans }}</span>
                        <select name="location" data-controller="autosubmit" data-action="change->autosubmit#submit">
                            <option value="all">{{ 'venue_context.all'|trans }}</option>
                            {% for venue in venue_context.venues %}
                                <option value="{{ venue.slug }}"{{ venue_context.location == venue.slug ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ venue.name }}</option>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </select>
                    </label>
                {% endif %}

                {% for field in _refine %}
                    {# A facet demoted out of the tile row keeps its tiles' shape.
                       The value of an option is what that tile would have put in
                       the query under this field's name — so the menu selects
                       exactly the rows the tile counted, and the count travels
                       with the label instead of being thrown away. #}
                    {% set _options = field.options|default(
                        field.tiles|default([])|map(tile => {
                            value: attribute(tile.query|default({}), field.name)|default(''),
                            label: (tile.label_is_key|default(false) ? tile.label|trans : tile.label)
                                   ~ (tile.count is defined ? ' (' ~ tile.count ~ ')' : ''),
                        })
                    ) %}
                    <label class="afp-select{{ field.value|default('') is not empty ? ' is-set' : '' }}">
                        <span class="afp-k">{{ field.label }}</span>
                        <select name="{{ field.name }}" data-controller="autosubmit" data-action="change->autosubmit#submit">
                            {% for option in _options %}
                                {# ⚠️ Loose `==` on purpose: ids arrive from the query
                                   string as strings and from the repository as ints. #}
                                <option value="{{ option.value }}"{{ field.value|default('') == option.value ? ' selected' : '' }}>{{ option.label }}</option>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </select>
                    </label>
                {% endfor %}

                {# Without JavaScript the dropdowns still submit — one extra click
                   rather than a dead control. Same contract as `autosubmit`. #}
                <noscript><button class="ml-btn" type="submit">{{ 'common.confirm'|trans }}</button></noscript>
            </form>
        {% endif %}
    </div>
{% endif %}
